  "query_preview": "(This evening through Friday)...\nIssued at 242 PM EDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nToday through Saturday night...\n\nA mid-level wave pushing through the region and moderate-strong \ndestabilization will continue to promote scattered convection \nthrough the evening. The disturbance is then expected to shift east \nallowing convection to diminish overnight. Severe weather is not \nexpected due to limited deep-layer wind shear. That said, forecast \nsoundings depict steep low-level lapse rates and DCAPE values up to \n1000 J/KG. This could support an isolated strong to severe downburst \nthis afternoon or evening. \n\nLatest CAMs have gradually become less bullish on the threat for a \nstrong downburst. This is likely due to early day convection and \nclouds limiting limiting destabilization more than initially expected. \nVery localized heavy rainfall cannot be ruled out either which may \nresult in flooding. Storms will be moving E or ENE around 20-30 mph, \nbut efficient rainfall rates and any brief training could result in \nlocalized flooding. \n\nAnother mid-upper level wave and an associated weak front will \nmove in on Saturday. Weak forcing from these features along with \nmoderate- strong destabilization may support isolated convection \nlate in the day. Convective development remains uncertain due to \nmarginal forcing for ascent, but if any strong storm can develop \nthen a strong downburst cannot be completely ruled out given \nsufficient DCAPE. \n\nSunday through next week...\n\nGuidance depicts longwave troughing across the region during much of \nthe period with multiple embedded shortwaves moving through within \nnorthwest flow aloft. These impulses will promote additional \nconvective chances at times as deeper moisture surges back into \ncentral Indiana. Expect daily rain and storms chances with low \nconfidence on exacts details due to varying model solutions \nregarding the low amplitude shortwaves passing through. The greatest \nprecipitation chances are from late Tuesday through Wednesday when \nthe strongest forcing and deeper moisture overlap. \n\nSome guidance is already hinting at MCSs or storm clusters \ndeveloping upstream and propagating towards Indiana at times. Look \nfor higher uncertainty regarding specific details of each subsequent \nround of convection. The evolution of previous day convection could \nsignificantly impact the forecast. In general, models depict strong \ndestabilization most days and enhancement of mid-upper level flow \nfrom the passing waves. This could potentially support isolated \nsevere thunderstorms somewhere near the region, but expect \nconfidence to remain low until models become better aligned. \nLocalized flooding from repeated rounds of convection and damaging \nwind gusts would likely be the primary threats from this setup.\n\nIt is worth noting CSU ML severe probabilities have increased \ngradually over the past few days and generally highlight the Sunday \nthrough Wednesday timeframe with at least low probabilities. Expect \nincreasingly warm and humid conditions late this weekend into early \ndue to warm air advection. The highest heat indices are expected on \nMonday when they could peak near 100F across much of central \nIndiana. These peak heat indices may not be reached if convection \nmoves through earlier in the day.",
